= ψ Phase Shifter =

{{Plain_Hook
| in_one_sentence    = A ψ phase shifter accumulates a voltage-controlled propagation phase <math>\varphi = k_z(\varepsilon_\psi(V)) \cdot L</math> in a BST-loaded waveguide section, providing the differential phase shift needed to steer ψ beams in [[PsiNet]] phased arrays and synchronise operators in multi-node coherence sessions.
| everyday_analogy   = A trombone player changes the pitch by adjusting the tube length — more tube means more delay and lower pitch. A ψ phase shifter does the same thing electronically: by changing the BST dielectric constant with a voltage, it adjusts the effective electrical length of the waveguide section, adding or subtracting phase from the ψ wave without changing its frequency or amplitude.
| what_it_predicts   = (1) A differential phase shift proportional to the BST tunability and the waveguide section length; (2) A figure of merit FOM = Δφ/insertion_loss that is maximised by a low-loss BST composite operating far above cutoff frequency; (3) A switching time determined by the RC time constant of the BST bias circuit — typically < 1 ms for head-worn scales; (4) A phase noise floor set by BST voltage noise, constraining PsiNet synchronisation accuracy.
| if_you_remember_one_thing = The phase shift is proportional to both the BST tunability and the waveguide length L — longer sections give more phase shift per volt, but also more insertion loss. The FOM = Δφ/loss is the engineering figure of merit that balances these competing effects.
}}

== Mathematical Origin ==

The propagation phase of the ψ₁₀ guided mode over length <math>L</math>:

:<math>\varphi = k_z(\omega,\varepsilon_\psi)\,L = \sqrt{\omega^2 - (\pi/a)^2 - m_{\rm eff}^2\varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi}\,L</math>

When BST bias changes <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math> by <math>\Delta\varepsilon_\psi</math>:

:<math>\Delta\varphi \approx \frac{\partial k_z}{\partial\varepsilon_\psi}\Delta\varepsilon_\psi \cdot L = \frac{-m_{\rm eff}^2\mu_\psi}{2k_z}\Delta\varepsilon_\psi \cdot L</math>

In the high-frequency limit (<math>k_z \approx \omega/v_g</math>):

:<math>\boxed{\Delta\varphi \approx -\frac{\omega L}{2v_g}\frac{\Delta\varepsilon_\psi}{\varepsilon_\psi}}</math>

For the BST composite with 10% tunability (<math>\Delta\varepsilon_\psi/\varepsilon_\psi = 0.10</math>), waveguide length <math>L = 5</math> cm, at 40 kHz with <math>v_g \approx 0.97c</math>:

:<math>|\Delta\varphi| \approx \frac{2\pi \times 4\times10^4 \times 0.05}{2 \times 0.97c} \times 0.10 \approx 2.2\times10^{-8}\,\text{rad}</math>

This is an extremely small phase shift in natural units — because the ψ guided wavelength at 40 kHz is ~7500 m (from [[ψ Waveguide]] C2) and the phase shifter is only 5 cm long. To achieve a useful phase shift (say <math>\Delta\varphi = \pi/4 = 0.785</math> rad), the required length:

:<math>L_{\pi/4} = \frac{\pi/4}{|\Delta\varphi/L|} \approx \frac{0.785}{4.4\times10^{-7}} \approx 1.8\,\text{Mm}</math>

This is impractical — it reveals a fundamental limitation of the waveguide phase shifter at these frequencies. The very long guided wavelength means that realistic phase shifts require either: (a) a much higher operating frequency, (b) a resonant phase shifter (exploiting the steep phase slope near resonance), or (c) a free-space phase shifter where path length differences are set geometrically rather than by material tuning.

=== Resonant Phase Shifter ===

Near the resonant frequency of the [[ψ Resonator]] (C1), the phase of the transmitted signal changes by <math>\pi</math> over a bandwidth <math>\Delta\omega = \omega_0/Q_\psi</math>. The phase slope near resonance:

:<math>\left.\frac{d\varphi}{d\omega}\right|_{\omega=\omega_0} = \frac{2Q_\psi}{\omega_0}</math>

At Q = 200 and 40 kHz: slope = <math>200/(\pi \times 4\times10^4) \approx 1.6</math> ms/rad. A frequency detuning of <math>\Delta\omega/(2\pi) = 100</math> Hz produces phase shift:

:<math>\Delta\varphi = \frac{2Q_\psi}{\omega_0}\Delta\omega = \frac{2\times200}{2\pi\times4\times10^4} \times 2\pi\times100 \approx 1\,\text{rad}</math>

A 1-radian phase shift from 100 Hz frequency detuning — achievable within the BST bias tuning range (which shifts resonant frequency by ±2 kHz from [[ψ Resonator]] C1). The resonant phase shifter is far more practical than the waveguide phase shifter at these frequencies.

== Implementation and Performance ==

The resonant phase shifter is implemented as a [[ψ Resonator]] (C1) operated slightly off resonance. The BST bias voltage shifts the resonant frequency, which shifts the phase of the transmitted ψ signal. Phase-shift range: <math>-\pi/2</math> to <math>+\pi/2</math> (90°) within the resonator bandwidth.

'''Figure of merit:''' <math>\mathrm{FOM} = \Delta\varphi/\mathrm{IL}_{\rm dB}</math> where IL is insertion loss in dB. For the resonant configuration: FOM ≈ 90°/0.5 dB = 180°/dB — competitive with EM voltage-controlled phase shifters.

'''Switching time:''' BST RC time constant at 200V bias, 10 pF equivalent capacitance, 1 kΩ drive resistance: <math>\tau_{\rm RC} = 10^{-11}\times10^3 = 10\,\text{ns}</math> — fast enough for all PsiNet synchronisation applications.

== ψ-Dependent Extensions ==

Phase synchronisation across a [[PsiNet]] cluster of N operators requires each operator's [[Psi Stabilizer]] to emit ψ at the same phase. The phase shifter provides the individual-operator phase trim to achieve collective coherence. Phase errors below <math>\pi/N</math> are required for N-operator constructive interference — at N = 10 this requires phase accuracy of 18°, achievable with the resonant phase shifter.

